<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>What the umpire does</h2>
<p>Under the typical appraisal clause, the two appraisers choose the umpire. If they cannot agree, either side can usually ask a court to appoint one. The umpire’s job is narrow: review the items the appraisers could not resolve and join one of them, or both, in signing an award on those items.</p>
<h2>How we run an umpire assignment</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Conflict check.</strong> We disclose any prior relationship with either appraiser, the carrier or the property owner before accepting.</li>
<li><strong>Scope of submission.</strong> We confirm which items are in dispute and which have already been agreed.</li>
<li><strong>Submissions and inspection.</strong> Each appraiser provides an estimate and supporting documents. We inspect the property when it helps.</li>
<li><strong>Hearing or conference.</strong> Most matters are handled informally; larger ones may use a structured meeting.</li>
<li><strong>Itemized award.</strong> We prepare a clear award form showing each item and its value.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Impartiality</h2>
<p>An umpire is only useful if both sides trust the process. We keep communications transparent, share materials with both appraisers and avoid one-sided contact on substantive issues.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who pays the umpire?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Usually the two sides split the umpire’s fee equally, as most appraisal clauses provide.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can the umpire decide coverage?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. The umpire, like the appraisers, decides the amount of loss on items submitted to the panel.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if the appraisers cannot agree on an umpire?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Most policies allow either party to ask a court to appoint one. Connecticut courts can be asked to do so.</p></div></details></div>
